Someone in the neighborhood is coordinating supply drops, and their problem is matching up volunteers with packages. The rule is straightforward: each package must go with exactly one volunteer and no package gets duplicated or ignored. Volunteers can take multiple packages but only up to their own weight allowance, so the combined weight they carry must stay under that cap. Since every package–volunteer combination has a cost attached, the goal is to put packages with volunteers so the total of those costs is as low as possible — just sum the costs for every chosen pairing and prefer the plan with the smallest sum, keeping each person’s load within their limit. All the specific weights and costs are listed below.

